\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The real irreducible commutant from adjunction}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Let a finite group #{G} act irreducibly on a nonzero
finite-dimensional real vector space #{V}. Then its
\vocabk{commutant}{fdip-003T}, with composition as multiplication,
is isomorphic as a real algebra to exactly one of
##{\mathbb R,\qquad\mathbb C,\qquad\mathbb H.}
For an invariant positive inner product, adjunction corresponds
to the usual conjugation in these algebras. This statement
has real coefficients.}

\proof{
\p{Sum any positive inner product over the finite group.
Every nonzero vector has a strictly positive contribution,
and reindexing the sum proves invariance. A commuting map
has invariant kernel and image; irreducibility makes a
nonzero such map invertible. Thus the commutant #{\mathcal C}
is a finite-dimensional real division algebra. Adjunction
preserves it, since the group acts orthogonally.}

\p{A self-adjoint element of #{\mathcal C} has a real
orthogonal eigenspace decomposition by \ref{fdip-002R}.
Each eigenspace is invariant under #{G}, so irreducibility
leaves one eigenvalue. Every self-adjoint commuting map is
therefore scalar. Decompose an arbitrary element into
self-adjoint and skew-adjoint parts to get
##{\mathcal C=\mathbb R\bbI\oplus\mathfrak a,\qquad
 \mathfrak a=\{a\in\mathcal C:a^\dagger=-a\}.}
For #{a\ne 0} in #{\mathfrak a}, the operator
#{a^\dagger a=-a^2} is a positive scalar multiple of the
identity. Polarization defines a positive inner product on
#{\mathfrak a} by
##{-\tfrac{1}{2}(ab+ba)=\langle a,b\rangle_{\mathfrak a}\bbI.}
The right side is scalar because the squares of #{a}, #{b}
and #{a+b} are scalar; bilinearity and positivity follow
from this formula and #{a^\dagger a}.}

\p{If #{\mathfrak a=0}, the algebra is #{\mathbb R}.
If its dimension is one, a unit vector #{u} has
#{u^2=-\bbI}, giving #{\mathbb C}. Otherwise choose
orthonormal #{u,v}. Then
#{u^2=v^2=-\bbI} and #{uv=-vu}. The element #{w=uv}
is skew-adjoint, satisfies #{w^2=-\bbI}, and anticommutes
with #{u,v}. Thus #{u,v,w} are orthonormal.}

\p{Let #{z\in\mathfrak a} be perpendicular to those three.
It anticommutes with each. Anticommuting with #{u} and #{v}
also makes it commute with #{uv=w}; together these relations
give #{2wz=0}. Since #{2} and #{w} are invertible, #{z=0}.
The skew subspace is therefore exactly three-dimensional.
The products of #{u,v,w} are the Hamilton quaternion rules,
giving #{\mathbb H}. Adjunction fixes the scalar part and
negates the skew part in all three cases, so it is the
stated conjugation. Their real dimensions #{1,2,4}
distinguish the algebras.}
}

\p{This proves the real classification used in
\ref{fdip-004Z} directly from the finite real spectral theorem.
For its representation-theoretic setting, compare
\citet{§13.2, pp. 106–109}{serre1977linear}.
No trichotomy over arbitrary coefficient rings is asserted.}
